    description: 'The land walls are the main event: a continuous rampart climbing from Fort Bokar at the sea, up and over the ridge through Minčeta, then down past the Buža Gate to the Ploče Gate and Revelin. This is the side an army could reach, so this is where the Republic put its mass.\n\nThe core dates from the 13th century, after the statute of 1272 laid out the city plan. From the 14th to the 16th century the walls were raised and thickened again and again until they stood up to 25 metres high and four to six metres thick, with a wall-walk wide enough for guns and fourteen square towers plus three round ones along the circuit.\n\nThe merlons and the crenellated parapet you walk behind today are largely late-medieval and Renaissance work, pierced with embrasures for small guns.',
    howItWorked: 'Height keeps out ladders and lets defenders see over the ground in front; thickness stops shot. Towers spaced along the wall let archers and later gunners fire sideways along the wall face, so that no stretch was a blind spot. The wall-walk runs unbroken so that reserves could be rushed to any threatened sector without descending into the streets.\n\nOn the north side the ground outside rises towards Srđ, which is why this is the tallest, thickest stretch and why Minčeta sits at its highest point. The outer wall and moat in front, added in the 15th century, completed the system.',
