    description: 'Along the southern side of the city the walls run on top of cliffs that drop straight into the Adriatic. Here the masonry is noticeably thinner, between one and a half and three metres, rising to about five where the ground allows an approach.\n\nThe reason is simple. No army could scale the cliffs and no 15th-century ship could carry guns heavy enough to breach a wall from a rolling deck. The sea itself was the moat, and the rock was the outer wall. The money saved here went into the land front.\n\nThe sea walls are the oldest part of the circuit in spirit: the original settlement on the islet of Laus looked out over this same water, and its first limestone ramparts in the 8th century stood along this edge.',
