    description: 'The Old Port is the reason Dubrovnik exists. The first settlement grew around a late-antique castle on this harbour, and the Republic\'s wealth came in through it. The three huge arches of the Great Arsenal, where galleys were built and repaired from the 12th century, still open onto the quay.\n\nIn 1485 the engineer Paskoje Miličević built the Kaše breakwater, a free-standing wall of huge stone blocks laid on a timber foundation without mortar, just outside the harbour mouth. It breaks the south-easterly seas and narrows the entrance so that the harbour chain could be shorter.\n\nThe Porporela, the long mole with the little lighthouse under St John\'s, is Austrian work of 1873 and is now the city\'s favourite evening promenade.',
    howItWorked: 'The harbour is the only place where an enemy could reach the city without climbing a wall, so it was treated as a gate. Every night the chain went up between St John\'s and the Kaše, and in time of alarm the arches of the Arsenal were walled shut so the fleet could not be burned. Guns on St John\'s and Revelin cross their fire over the entrance; the Kaše forces incoming boats into a narrow, predictable channel under those guns.\n\nInside, the port is sealed from the city by its own wall, pierced only by the Ponte Gate and the Fishmarket Gate, so even a successful landing would still face a second line.',
