    description: 'The Stradun is the limestone spine of the Old Town, 300 metres from the Pile Gate to the clock tower at Ploče. It is also the join between two cities. Ragusa, the Roman-Greek refugee town, sat on a rocky islet to the south; Dubrava, the Slavic settlement, sat on the wooded slope to the north. A marshy sea channel ran between them.\n\nIn the 12th and 13th centuries the channel was filled in, the two towns became one, and the filled strip became the main street. The statute of 1272 fixed the grid of lanes that run off it.\n\nThe street you see is Baroque. The earthquake of 6 April 1667 destroyed every house along it; the Republic then passed a law dictating the plan of every new building, which is why the Stradun is so uniform, each house a shop below with a door and window in one arched frame, living rooms above, and the kitchen in the attic to keep fire away from the street.',
    howItWorked: 'The Stradun is not a defensive work, but it is part of the system. The whole street plan was laid out for fast movement between the two gates and the forts, so that the small garrison could reach any threatened point in minutes. The uniform stone houses, with kitchens in the attic and shops shuttered at night, were also a fire-prevention scheme in a city that had learned the hard way what fire does after an earthquake.',
    underFire: 'The Stradun was the bull\'s-eye of the 6 December 1991 bombardment: more than 600 shells and missiles fell on the Old Town between dawn and late morning, concentrated on this street and the blocks to its north-east. Shells cratered the paving, fires gutted several palaces and the Inter-University Centre library, and the polished limestone was pitted along its length. Almost all of the damage has since been repaired; look for the brighter new roof tiles to see where the shells fell.',
