    description: 'Srđ is the bare limestone ridge that rises 412 metres directly behind the city. It is close enough that a gun on top can drop a shell into any street in the Old Town, which is why, for most of Dubrovnik\'s history, the question was never "can the walls hold?" but "who holds the hill?"\n\nThe Russians and Montenegrins answered that in 1806 by putting their batteries on Srđ and bombarding the French-held city. The French learned the lesson and built Fort Imperial on the summit, a low, thick-walled Napoleonic fort begun in 1806 and finished in 1812, named for the Emperor. The Austrians later added to it.\n\nIn 1991 Fort Imperial was held by a handful of Croatian soldiers, and the whole siege turned on their refusal to leave.',
    howItWorked: 'From the Republic\'s point of view, Srđ was the great flaw in an otherwise perfect position. The medieval walls were designed against attackers on the ground in front of them, not against plunging fire from a hill 400 metres up. Ragusa\'s solution was diplomacy: make sure nobody hostile ever got to stand on it. Napoleon\'s solution was the fort. The Croatians\' solution in 1991 was to hold the fort with almost nothing, because if the JNA had taken Srđ it could have placed observers and guns directly over the city and the Old Town would have been indefensible.',
    underFire: 'On 6 December 1991 Fort Imperial came under attack at 5:50 a.m., minutes after the bombardment of the Old Town began. A JNA company with tank and artillery support reached the fort by 8 a.m.; the defenders retreated inside and called for help, and the Croatian commander Nojko Marinović ordered his own artillery to fire directly onto the fortress to drive the attackers off its walls, while a special police unit fought its way up to reinforce it. By 2 p.m. the JNA called off the assault. The fort was shelled and bombed from the air repeatedly through October and November but never fell.',
