    description: 'Lokrum is the wooded island that fills the view from the sea walls, just 600 metres from the Old Port. Benedictine monks founded an abbey here in 1023, the first written mention of the island, and farmed exotic fruit; the name comes from the Latin <em>acrumen</em>, sour fruit.\n\nLegend says Richard the Lionheart was wrecked here in 1192 on his way home from the Third Crusade and vowed to build a church in thanks; the Ragusans persuaded him to build it in the city instead. Another legend says the last monks, evicted in the 19th century, walked the island by night with upturned candles and cursed anyone who should ever own it, and the island\'s later owners, including the ill-fated Archduke Maximilian of Mexico, did their best to prove the point.\n\nOn the summit the French built Fort Royal from 1806, finished by the Austrians in 1835 and later nicknamed Maximilian\'s Tower.',
