    description: 'On 27 July 1377 the Great Council of Ragusa passed a law that no ship or traveller from a plague-infected place could enter the city until they had spent a month in isolation on the offshore islets of Mrkan, Bobara and Supetar. It is among the earliest quarantine laws anywhere, and the <em>trentina</em> of thirty days later stretched to forty: a <em>quarantina</em>.\n\nThe isolation stations moved closer to the city over the next two centuries, to Danče and then to Lokrum, until in 1590 work began on a permanent complex just outside the Ploče Gate. Built in stages and complete by the 1640s, the Lazareti is a chain of ten long stone halls around five courtyards, five for goods and five for people, with guardhouses at each end and lodgings for the Ottoman envoy who judged disputes among the Turkish merchants held there.\n\nIt is the only almost completely preserved quarantine of its type on the eastern Mediterranean.',
    howItWorked: 'The Lazareti is a wall against disease, placed exactly where the land trade arrived. Caravans from Bosnia and the Ottoman Balkans came down the coast road to Ploče; their goods were unloaded through doors facing the sea and stacked under arcades, their merchants lodged in the small houses on the upper courtyard, and everyone was watched by guards and health officers, the <em>kacamorti</em>, for the full term. Only then did the Ploče Gate open. The site sits under the guns of Revelin, so it also served as a controlled holding area if any caravan turned out to be more than merchants.',
    underFire: 'The complex was damaged by fires in the 19th century and again at the end of the First World War, and used as barracks after the fall of the Republic. In 1991 the Ploče suburb around it was shelled from the first week of October. It has since been restored as an arts and community quarter.',
